\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Data}
\label{sec:data}

We use the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench}, which aggregates binary (correct/incorrect) evaluation results from the Open LLM Leaderboard. For MMLU~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, this yields a sparse matrix of 5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 items spanning model releases from 2023 through 2024. Each entry $x_{ij} \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates whether model $i$ answered item $j$ correctly. As an independent reference for contamination status, we use the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination}, which classify 9,229 of 12,508 MMLU items (73.8\% coverage) as ``contaminated'' or ``clean'' based on verbatim matches in publicly accessible web corpora.

\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF and ETS Classification}
\label{sec:mhdif}

Differential Item Functioning (DIF) occurs when an item's statistical relationship to overall ability differs between two groups of examinees, after conditioning on ability level. In educational testing, DIF detects items that function unfairly across demographic groups. We repurpose this framework for LLM benchmarks: ``examinees'' become models, ``test items'' become benchmark questions, and the two groups (reference and focal cohorts) are defined by temporal or structural criteria.

The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure~\citep{holland1988mh} stratifies models into $K$ bins by total score and computes a common odds ratio across strata. Within each stratum $k$, the $2 \times 2$ contingency table has entries $A_k$ (reference correct), $B_k$ (reference incorrect), $C_k$ (focal correct), and $D_k$ (focal incorrect), with stratum total $T_k$. The MH estimator is
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\mathrm{MH}} = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{K} A_k D_k / T_k}{\sum_{k=1}^{K} B_k C_k / T_k},
\label{eq:mh}
\end{equation}
which is converted to the $\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}$ scale via $\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}} = -2.35 \ln(\alpha_{\mathrm{MH}})$. Positive $\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}$ indicates the item favors the reference group; negative values favor the focal group.

The Educational Testing Service (ETS) classification assigns each item to one of three categories based on $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|$: A (negligible DIF, $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| < 1.0$), B (moderate DIF, $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| < 1.5$), and C (large DIF, $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ \emph{and} $p < 0.05$ on the MH chi-square test). The ETS~C category requires both a large effect size and statistical significance, imposing a dual threshold that provides implicit protection against false positives.

\subsection{Cohort Definitions}
\label{sec:cohorts}

We apply MH-DIF under five cohort designs that vary the source of group differences:

\paragraph{Temporal cohort.} Models released in 2023 ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$) versus 2024 ($N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$). This is the primary comparison for measuring temporal item instability.

\paragraph{Ability-quintile cohort.} The lowest-scoring models (quintiles 1--2) versus the highest-scoring models (quintiles 4--5) within the same temporal window. This design isolates ability-driven DIF from temporal effects.

\paragraph{Within-family cohort.} Llama-2 ($N = 161$) versus Llama-3 ($N = 308$) models. This comparison within a single model family exposes the sensitivity of MH-DIF to ability confounding, since successive generations within a family exhibit large ability gaps.

\paragraph{Within-subject cohort.} MH-DIF is run separately for each of 57 MMLU subjects, using within-subject total score as the matching variable. This tests whether temporal instability persists within homogeneous knowledge domains.

\paragraph{Random-split (sanity check).} The 2023 cohort is split into two random halves of equal size. Under the null hypothesis of no DIF, ETS~C rates should be near zero.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Injection Framework}
\label{sec:injection}

To create controlled ground truth for evaluating DIF detection, we inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix. The procedure selects 20\% of items that are classified ETS~A (negligible DIF) in the uninjected data as the ``contaminated'' target set. For each target item, incorrect responses in the focal group are flipped to correct at an exploitation rate $p \in \{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$. MH-DIF is then run on the modified matrix, and detection performance is measured as
\begin{equation}
\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = \mathrm{TPR}(p) - \mathrm{TPR}(0), \qquad \Delta\mathrm{FPR} = \mathrm{FPR}(p) - \mathrm{FPR}(0),
\end{equation}
where TPR is the fraction of target items classified ETS~C and FPR is the fraction of non-target items classified ETS~C. Five random seeds are used per injection level for uncertainty estimation.

The choice of matching variable is critical. When the matching variable (total score) is computed from all items including the contaminated subset, the matching variable itself absorbs part of the injection signal. This \emph{matching variable contamination} inflates FPR because models that benefit from the injection are assigned to higher-ability strata, where the contamination effect is diluted. We introduce \textbf{cross-subject matching}: the MH matching variable for items in subject $s$ is computed as the total score on all subjects \emph{except} $s$. This ensures that the conditioning variable is unaffected by the injected contamination, eliminating FPR inflation ($\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all tested subjects). We adopt cross-subject matching as the default protocol and recommend it for semi-synthetic DIF evaluation on any multi-domain benchmark.

\subsection{Diagnostic Analyses}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Three diagnostic analyses characterize the sources and structure of the observed DIF.

\paragraph{Local independence (Yen's Q3).} The MH procedure assumes local independence: conditional on the latent trait, item responses should be uncorrelated. Yen's Q3 statistic measures residual correlations between item pairs after fitting a unidimensional IRT model. Elevated Q3 values within subjects (relative to cross-subject pairs) indicate local dependence that could inflate DIF rates.

\paragraph{Domain-specificity analysis.} We compute the Spearman correlation between subject-level C\% and subject-level accuracy improvement from 2023 to 2024. A positive correlation would suggest that domains with larger capability gains exhibit more instability. Item-level logistic regression decomposes the C\% into contributions from domain-level accuracy improvement versus item-specific factors.

\paragraph{Downstream impact.} We evaluate whether DIF-flagged items carry unique information for model evaluation by comparing Spearman rank correlations between full-benchmark and reduced-benchmark rankings (removing DIF items vs.\ removing equal-sized random subsets). A circularity ablation compares DIF-based removal against alternative item selection strategies (accuracy gap, discrimination, variance) to test whether the DIF effect is unique or replicable by simpler heuristics.

\subsection{Statistical Considerations}
\label{sec:statistical}

The ETS~C dual threshold (effect size $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ and significance $p < 0.05$) provides implicit control of the false positive rate. As a robustness check, we apply Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ to all 12,508 item-level $p$-values and reclassify items using the corrected significance threshold. We also report results across a range of effect-size thresholds ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \in \{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\}$) to assess sensitivity to the classification boundary.

The METABENCH MMLU population is 99.98\% decoder-only (5,226 of 5,227 models). We verify that architecture heterogeneity does not confound DIF results by repeating the temporal analysis on the decoder-only subset. A random-split sanity check (expected C\% $\approx 0$) validates that the MH-DIF procedure does not spuriously flag items in the absence of true group differences.

